##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1525 1451  230
                                                                  1525 1451  231
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1525 1451  232
                                                                  1525 1451  233
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1525 1451  234
                                                                  1525 1451  235
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1525 1451  236
                                                                  1525 1451  237
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1525 1451  238
                                                                  1525 1451  239
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1525 1451  240
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1525 1451  241
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1525 1451  242
                                                                  1525 1451  243
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1525 1451  244
                                                                  1525 1451  245
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1525 1451  246
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1525 1451  247
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1525 1451  248
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1525 1451  249
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1525 1451  250
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1525 1451  251
to the highest incident energy.                                   1525 1451  252
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1525 1451  253
                                                                  1525 1451  254
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1525 1451  255
                                                                  1525 1451  256
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1525 1451  257
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1525 1451  258
                                                                  1525 1451  259
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1525 1451  260
                                                                  1525 1451  261
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1525 1451  262
total cross section.                                              1525 1451  263
                                                                  1525 1451  264
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1525 1451  265
                                                                  1525 1451  266
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1525 1451  267
                                                                  1525 1451  268
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1525 1451  269
                                                                  1525 1451  270
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1525 1451  271
                                                                  1525 1451  272
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1525 1451  273
                                                                  1525 1451  274
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1525 1451  275
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1525 1451  276
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1525 1451  277
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1525 1451  278
sections.                                                         1525 1451  279
                                                                  1525 1451  280
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1525 1451  281
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1525 1451  282
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1525 1451  283
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1525 1451  284
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1525 1451  285
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1525 1451  286
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1525 1451  287
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1525 1451  288
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1525 1451  289
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1525 1451  290
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1525 1451  291
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1525 1451  292
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1525 1451  293
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1525 1451  294
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1525 1451  295
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1525 1451  296
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1525 1451  297
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1525 1451  298
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1525 1451  299
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1525 1451  300
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1525 1451  301
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1525 1451  302
                                                                  1525 1451  303
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1525 1451  304
                                                                  1525 1451  305
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1525 1451  306
discrete levels.                                                  1525 1451  307
                                                                  1525 1451  308
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1525 1451  309
                                                                  1525 1451  310
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1525 1451  311
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1525 1451  312
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1525 1451  313
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1525 1451  314
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1525 1451  315
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1525 1451  316
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1525 1451  317
on an angular grid.                                               1525 1451  318
                                                                  1525 1451  319
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1525 1451  320
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1525 1451  321
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1525 1451  322
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1525 1451  323
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1525 1451  324
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1525 1451  325
                                                                  1525 1451  326
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1525 1451  327
                                                                  1525 1451  328
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1525 1451  329
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1525 1451  330
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1525 1451  331
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1525 1451  332
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1525 1451  333
                                                                  1525 1451  334
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1525 1451  335
                                                                  1525 1451  336
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1525 1451  337
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1525 1451  338
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1525 1451  339
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1525 1451  340
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1525 1451  341
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1525 1451  342
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1525 1451  343
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1525 1451  344
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1525 1451  345
For each residual product:                                        1525 1451  346
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1525 1451  347
- the recoil                                                      1525 1451  348
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1525 1451  349
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1525 1451  350
sections.                                                         1525 1451  351
                                                                  1525 1451  352
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1525 1451  353
                                                                  1525 1451  354
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1525 1451  355
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1525 1451  356
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1525 1451  357
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1525 1451  358
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1525 1451  359
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1525 1451  360
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1525 1451  361
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1525 1451  362
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1525 1451  363
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1525 1451  364
                                                                  1525 1451  365
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1525 1451  366
followed:                                                         1525 1451  367
                                                                  1525 1451  368
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1525 1451  369
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1525 1451  370
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1525 1451  371
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1525 1451  372
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1525 1451  373
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1525 1451  374
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1525 1451  375
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1525 1451  376
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1525 1451  377
                                                                  1525 1451  378
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1525 1451  379
                                                                  1525 1451  380
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1525 1451  381
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1525 1451  382
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1525 1451  383
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1525 1451  384
                                                                  1525 1451  385
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1525 1451  386
                                                                  1525 1451  387
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1525 1451  388
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1525 1451  389
as isomer.                                                        1525 1451  390
                                                                  1525 1451  391
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1525 1451  392
                                                                  1525 1451  393
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1525 1451  394
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1525 1451  395
as isomer.                                                        1525 1451  396
                                                                  1525 1451  397
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1525 1451  398
                                                                  1525 1451  399
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1525 1451  400
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1525 1451  401
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1525 1451  402
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1525 1451  403
  given                                                           1525 1451  404
                                                                  1525 1451  405
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1525 1451  406
                                                                  1525 1451  407
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1525 1451  408
                                                                  1525 1451  409
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1525 1451  410
                                                                  1525 1451  411
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1525 1451  412
                                                                  1525 1451  413
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1525 1451  414
                                                                  1525 1451  415
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1525 1451  416
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1525 1451  417
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1525 1451  418
                                                                  1525 1451  419
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1525 1451  420
                                                                  1525 1451  421
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1525 1451  422
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1525 1451  423
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1525 1451  424
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1525 1451  425
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1525 1451  426
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1525 1451  427
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1525 1451  428
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1525 1451  429
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1525 1451  430
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1525 1451  431
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1525 1451  432
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1525 1451  433
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1525 1451  434
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1525 1451  435
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1525 1451  436
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1525 1451  437
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1525 1451  438
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1525 1451  439
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1525 1451  440
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1525 1451  441
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1525 1451  442
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1525 1451  443
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1525 1451  444
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1525 1451  445
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1525 1451  446
                                                                  1525 1451  447
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1525 1451  448
                                                                  1525 1451  449
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1525 1451  450
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1525 1451  451
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1525 1451  452
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1525 1451  453
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1525 1451  454
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1525 1451  455
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1525 1451  456
representation is used.                                           1525 1451  457
                                                                  1525 1451  458
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1525 1451  459
                                                                  1525 1451  460
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1525 1451  461
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1525 1451  462
                                                                  1525 1451  463
